%[text] # OPT 212: Solving Nonlinear Equations: Newton's method
%[text] Solutions
%[text] These exercises accompany the Solving Nonlinear Equations active script. They focus on bracketing, bisection, Newton iteration, and ray intersection with a curved surface.
%[text] 
clear
close all
clc
format compact
set(groot,'DefaultAxesFontSize',14,'DefaultTextFontSize',14, ...
    'DefaultLegendFontSize',14,'DefaultColorbarFontSize',14);
%%
%[text] ## Example -- simple brute force search for a polynomial
%[text] For the brute force search, we simply sample the search region and look for zero crossings
format long e
x = linspace(0,2,10003);
dx = x(2) - x(1); %distance between values of x
y = @(x) 2.*x.^3 + x.^2 - pi.*x - 2; 
yy = y(x);
signtest = yy(1:end-1).*yy(2:end);
lz = signtest<=0; 
xz = x(lz) + dx/2;
y(xz);
figure %[output:05f7648a]
plot(x,y(x),'-r','LineWidth',1.2); hold on %[output:05f7648a]
plot(xz,zeros(size(xz)),'x','MarkerSize',12) %[output:05f7648a]
plot([min(x) max(x)],[0 0],'--b') %[output:05f7648a]
hold off %[output:05f7648a]
ylabel('function value') %[output:05f7648a]
xlabel('x') %[output:05f7648a]
legend('function','roots') %[output:05f7648a]

%[text] ## 
%[text] ## 
%[text] ## Exercise 1: Brute force search for a Fringe Level
%[text] A normalized two-beam interference signal can be written
%[text]{"align":"center"} $I(z)={1\\over 2}\\left\[1+\\cos\\left({2\\pi z\\over p}+\\phi\\right)\\right\].$
%[text] Tasks:
%[text] 1\. Use a fringe period `p = 3.0 mm`, phase `0.40 rad`, and threshold irradiance `0.80` in normalized arbitrary units. 
%[text] 2\. Plot the function.
%[text] 3\. Locate the points at which the Irradiance crosses the threshold.
%[text] Your result should look something like this:
%[text] ![](text:image:5926) 
% Insert code here
% Define parameters
period_mm = 3.0;
phase = 0.40;
threshold = 0.80;
fringe = @(z) 0.5*(1+cos(2*pi*z/period_mm + phase));
f = @(z) fringe(z) - threshold;
% Choose sampling points
z = linspace(0,3,501);
% Sample the function
ff = f(z);
% Perform logical test for threshold
signtest = ff(1:end-1).*ff(2:end);
lz = signtest <=0;
z0 = (z(1:end-1)+z(2:end))/2;
z0 = z0(lz) % identify the zeros %[output:5b605de5]

figure %[output:97a2311e]
plot(z,fringe(z),'b-','LineWidth',1.4) %[output:97a2311e]
hold on %[output:97a2311e]
yline(threshold,'r--','LineWidth',1.2) %[output:97a2311e]
plot(z0,fringe(z0),'ko','MarkerFaceColor','k') %[output:97a2311e]
hold off %[output:97a2311e]
grid on %[output:97a2311e]
xlabel('z (mm)') %[output:97a2311e]
ylabel('normalized irradiance') %[output:97a2311e]
title('Brute force search for fringe thresholds'); %[output:97a2311e]
%%
%[text] 
%[text] **Newton's method** uses a local Taylor expansion. If $x\_n$ is the current estimate, then
%[text]{"align":"center"} $g(x\_n+h)\\approx g(x\_n)+h\\,g'(x\_n).$
%[text] Setting this linear approximation equal to zero gives
%[text]{"align":"center"} $h=-\\frac{g(x\_n)}{g'(x\_n)},$
%[text] and therefore the iteration
%[text]{"align":"center"} $x\_{n+1}=x\_n-\\frac{g(x\_n)}{g'(x\_n)}.$
%[text] Newton's method can converge very rapidly near a well-behaved root, but the notes emphasize that it is not automatically robust. A poor starting point or a misleading local slope can send the iteration far from the intended solution. A practical strategy is to use a brute-force search to obtain starting estimates, then refine them with Newton's method.
%[text] ## Exercise 2: Newton's Method for the Grating Equation
%[text] A transmission grating obeys
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\Lambda(\\sin\\theta\_m-\\sin\\theta\_i)=m\\lambda,$
%[text] where $\\Lambda$represents the grating period, $\\theta\_i$ represents the angle of incidence, $\\theta\_m$ the angle of diffraction, $\\lambda$ the wavelength and $m$ the 'order' of the diffraction.
%[text] There is, of course, an exact solution.  You can get there by solving the linear equation for $\\sin\\theta\_m$.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\sin\\theta\_m=\\frac{m\\lambda}{\\Lambda} + \\sin\\theta\_i$
%[text] and then solving for the arcsin (inverse sin) using the asin function.   
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\theta\_m=\\sin^{-1}\\left(\\frac{m\\lambda}{\\Lambda} + \\sin\\theta\_i\\right)$
%[text] This has a built-in function in MatLab but we will use Newton's method and check our accuracy.  To do so, we first define it so we are finding the roots: 
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\frac{m\\lambda}{\\Lambda} + \\sin\\theta\_i - \\sin\\theta\_m= 0$
%[text] Use wavelength `633 nm`, grating density `600 lines/mm`, incident angle `10 deg`, and order `m = 1`. Convert the wavelength from nm to mm before combining it with the groove spacing.  Pay attention to the units of angle:  If you use an angle of degrees you will need to use the sind function in MatLab.  If you use radians, you can use the sin function.   
%[text] Tasks:
%[text] 1\. Convert grooves per mm to a period $\\Lambda$.  
%[text] 2\. Choose a starting value based on the small angle approximation.    
%[text] 3\. Use Newton's method to solve for the angle of the first diffraction order. 
%[text] 4\. Plot the residual magnitude versus iteration number.
%[text] The result should look like this:
%[text] 
%[text] ![](text:image:12bd)

% Parameters
lambda_nm = 633;
lambda_mm = lambda_nm/1e6;
lines_per_mm = 600;
Lambda = 1/lines_per_mm;
m = 1;
theta_i = 10*pi/180;

% Function and derivative
g = @(theta) m*lambda_mm/Lambda + sin(theta_i) - sin(theta);
gprime = @(theta) -cos(theta);

% Initial guess from small angle approximation
theta = theta_i + m*lambda_mm/Lambda;

% Set tolerance and maximum iterations
tol = 1e-12;
max_iter = 20;

% Store initial residual
residual_history = abs(g(theta));

% Newton iteration
for iter = 1:max_iter
    slope = gprime(theta);

    if abs(slope) < 1e-12
        error('Derivative is too close to zero');
    end

    theta = theta - g(theta)/slope;

    residual_history(end+1) = abs(g(theta));

    if residual_history(end) < tol
        break
    end
end

% Convert result to degrees
theta_deg = theta*180/pi;

% Compare with exact solution
theta_exact = asin(m*lambda_mm/Lambda + sin(theta_i));

fprintf('Newton angle = %.6f degrees\n',theta_deg); %[output:6cc33f62]
fprintf('Exact angle = %.6f degrees\n',theta_exact*180/pi); %[output:181821fd]

% Plot convergence
figure %[output:8316635b]
semilogy(0:numel(residual_history)-1, residual_history, 'o-') %[output:8316635b]
grid on %[output:8316635b]
xlabel('Iteration number') %[output:8316635b]
ylabel('Residual magnitude') %[output:8316635b]
title('Newton Method for Grating Equation'); %[output:8316635b]



